Implementation Summary

The Python implementation packages five fictional loan-letter cases, constructs the complete rubric-based judge prompt, evaluates each candidate response, collects per-case score/reasoning/criteria/time/token/cost fields, calculates aggregate statistics, and writes a structured JSON receipt. Its online mode uses the OpenAI Python SDK with Pydantic structured output and the gpt-4o-mini model specified by the lab. The pricing constants used for optional cost estimation are documented in code and should be rechecked before a client run. Secrets are read only from the environment and are excluded by .gitignore.

Because no API key is required or committed, the submitted evaluation_results.json comes from the explicit offline calibration mode. That mode uses fixed candidate responses and fixed expected judgments, so it validates control flow, schema, timing, aggregation, reproducibility, and result export without pretending an API call occurred. The run produced a 3.6/5 mean, a 60% pass rate, 80% criteria attainment, zero billed tokens, and zero API cost. The two failing fixtures exposed an invented customer-facing credit reason and unequal treatment in a counterfactual pair; the missing-data case correctly blocked.

Before production use, the bank should run the online mode on a substantially larger versioned dataset, pin a model snapshot, repeat stochastic judgments, and compare judge decisions with independent human labels. A release gate should emphasize the false-pass rate on critical defects rather than average score alone. Public benchmark results can support diagnosis, but the private scenario set and human-validated ground truth should remain the primary deployment evidence
